  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>A table for the relation \(f\) is below. </para>
      <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
        <thead/>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
        1
      </entry>
            <entry>
        2
      </entry>
            <entry>
        3
      </entry>
            <entry>
        1
      </entry>
            <entry>
        4
      </entry>
            <entry>
        5
      </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>\(f(x)\)</emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
        -1
      </entry>
            <entry>
        5
      </entry>
            <entry>
        -2
      </entry>
            <entry>
        1
      </entry>
            <entry>
        3
      </entry>
            <entry>
        -2
      </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <!--Question 2 -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Is this relation a function? Explain your answer.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(f\) is not a function because \(f(1) =-1\) and \(f(1)=1 \).
      
      
    </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
        <item>Use the Desmos graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the points in the relation. 
    
    Interact with the graph. Graph the points in the relation on a coordinate grid.
  </item>
      </list>
      <div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>When you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="ALT TEXT: COORDINATE GRID FROM −1 TO 8 ON THE X-AXIS AND FROM −2 TO 6 ON THE Y-AXIS. THE POINTS (1, −1), (2, 5), (3, −2), (1, 1), (4, 3), AND (5, −2) ARE PLOTTED.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_08_03_01.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <div class="os-raise-ib-content" data-schema-version="1.0" data-wait-for-event="Reveal1"> </div>
      <!--Question 3 -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What do you notice about the points \((1, -1)\) and \((1,1)\)?
      </para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>They are on the same vertical line.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <para>VERTICAL LINE TEST</para>
      <para> A set of points in a rectangular coordinate system is the graph of a function if every vertical line intersects the graph in at most one point.</para>
      <para>If any vertical line intersects the graph in more than one point, the graph does not represent a function.</para>
      <para>If the graph does represent a function, we say it “passes the vertical line test.”</para>
      <!--Question Part A -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> For the function graphed below, determine if the graph passes the vertical line test and then explain how this identifies the graph as a function or not. </para>
          <figure>
            <media alt="The figure has two graphs. In graph a there is a straight line graphed on the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y-axes run from negative 10 to 10. The line goes through the points (0, 2), (3, 0), and (6, negative 2). In graph b there is a parabola opening to the right graphed on the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y-axes run from negative 6 to 6. The parabola goes through the points (negative 1, 0), (0, 1), (0, negative 1), (3, 2), and (3, negative 2).">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_08_03_02.png"/>
            </media>
          </figure>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Yes, this passes the vertical line test (see the sample blue lines below) for all points on the line so the graph is a function. </para>
          <figure>
            <media alt="ALT TEXT: THE GRAPH OF A STRAIGHT LINE THAT PASSES THROUGH THE POINTS (0, 2) AND (3, 0), SHOWN IN RED. A BLUE VERTICAL LINE PASSING THROUGH (−2, 0). A GREEN VERTICAL LINE PASSING THROUGH (4, 0).">
              <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_08_03_03.jpg"/>
            </media>
          </figure>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Question Part b -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>For the function graphed below, determine if the graph passes the vertical line test and then explain how this identifies the graph as a function or not.</para>
          <figure>
            <media alt="The figure has two graphs. In graph a there is a straight line graphed on the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y-axes run from negative 10 to 10. The line goes through the points (0, 2), (3, 0), and (6, negative 2). In graph b there is a parabola opening to the right graphed on the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y-axes run from negative 6 to 6. The parabola goes through the points (negative 1, 0), (0, 1), (0, negative 1), (3, 2), and (3, negative 2).">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_08_03_04.png"/>
            </media>
          </figure>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>No, this graph does not pass the vertical line test, so it is not a function. For example, the vertical line \(x=1\) crosses the graph more than once.</para>
          <figure>
            <media alt="A SIDEWAYS PARABOLA THAT OPENS TO THE RIGHT AND PASSES THROUGH THE POINTS (−1, 0), (3, 2), AND (3, −2). A BLACK VERTICAL LINE PASSING THROUGH (1, 0).">
              <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_08_03_05.jpg"/>
            </media>
          </figure>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <para>Notice that the graph of a non-vertical line will always be a function. This is called a <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">linear function. </term>. </para>
